        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Before embedding the coskewness decomposition in a tree, this section presents a limited simulation study that attests that the method based on coskewness can recover the inherent structure the covariance objective cannot, in a controlled setting. A predictor block of six columns is constructed in which a single column drives the response through its square, next to a weak linear decoy. Note that this is a purely third-order relationship. Let $z$ be a standard-Gaussian variable, $x_2$ a second, independent one, and $n_1, \\ldots, n_4$ four independent Gaussian noise columns; set $\\mathbf{X} = [z, x_2, n_1, n_2, n_3, n_4]$ and a single response",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                1122,
                1084,
                1326
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
